ng us today it's great to have  this opportunity  to talk to you about the work that you  and your colleagues are doing at health  data research network canada  to coordinate and integrate data across  the country to advance health research  let's start the conversation by having  you describe a little bit about  hdr and canada including its vision  and its partnership model great thanks  for the opportunity it's great to have  this conversation with you today  so health data research network canada  is a  newly incorporated not-for-profit and  it's a pan-canadian network  that is meant to facilitate and  accelerate multi-jurisdictional research  so our network includes data centers and  other organizations  representing all provinces and northwest  territory and we're working on bringing  in nunavut and uconn as well  plus statistics canada and the canadian  institute for health information are  kaihi  and the starting position of our network  and the reason we started working  together  over five years ago well before we had  any funding to actually do the kind of  work we're doing now  is that we we really looked across the  country and see that we have this  position in the world of data where the  glass really is  three-quarters full we've got lots of  strength in  our at the national level and in our  individual provinces and territories  but a lot of difficulty in actually  doing  a good deal of multi-jurisdictional  research and we really felt that if we  had  just the ability to add a little bit  more  we could leverage all of those great  existing resources and  and really promote a lot of more  innovative research  so we're a distributed network and we  have a commitment to distributing  funding we're not creating something  central  we're not trying to create a single  database  across the country and part of that is  because we have a principle of  respecting local context and local  policies  and taking comers as they come  and without demanding that they act a  certain way or be a certain thing in  order to be part of our network  and we're leveraging wherever possible  so we're not trying to build something  new if something  excellent already exists in the world of  data data resources and  access and so on so just very briefly we  have seven objectives overall for our  network  one is to create a data access support  system to help navigate  multi-jurisdictional researchers so it's  one-stop shopping one form  one request process we're working to  harmonize and validate definitions  for important chronic diseases and other  variables so we can have  apples-to-apples comparisons  of data across the country we're  continuing to expand the types of data  that are available  our bedrock really is administrative  data population-based information  but we can link that to cohorts to  surveys  to electronic medical records and other  things as they become available  we're developing technology  infrastructure to improve  the request process but also  documentation storage metadata and other  things really and this is an eye towards  sustainability of the network and the  functions  we're creating supports for advanced  analytics and  infrastructure for data analysis and  really focusing there on distributed  data analysis because we know this is  one of the ways of the future we're  establishing really strong partnerships  with  patients the public and indigenous  communities and this is really around  underlining the ethics of data use  particularly as data resources are  changing so much  and building strong governance to enable  national coordination  ways of working together that we have  the strength from everybody  so you can see that our work really is  around organizing  and making data available the technical  supports around that and really a strong  focus on  ethics and engagement and then of course  transparency and  sustainability it's an amazing  initiative  and it's so incredibly foundational to  our 