ernment and i mean  that's a whole political science  conversation but  i i do think it's important to  acknowledge that that is part of what's  going on here  the other is that because provinces and  territories have  the individual responsibility for  delivery of health care and other kinds  of services  the data to collect the way that they're  collected  and the technical systems that they use  to collect the data  even though the services that are  covered are different  and this really creates a challenge for  again the apples to apples comparison  but also  the the ability just to assemble and  then use the data appropriately  so having said all that the the  advantages that  come from addressing this  um it really  are are that we can we can look at that  the effect it's so it's a natural  laboratory in canada we have different  policies and different practices so we  can learn from those  if we can compare across jurisdictions  we can also at a national level look at  the effects of  small populations small small  number of conditions or people who have  had a  relatively um unique service and really  at the pan canadian level for those  small populations that's the only way we  can do some of this research  and so we do still have lots of  challenges including sharing of data  across  boundaries and at this point i'd also  say that we really haven't had enough  engagement with the public  around at the ethical issues of doing  the kind of data linkage and analysis  that we really want to do  great so and i know that you're working  hard on that front  and and you mentioned earlier the work  that you do in  engaging with indigenous communities as  well one thing that just occurred to me  as you were speaking kim i wonder if  um one of the other advantages of  integrating and coordinating  access to data across the country health  data in particular across the country  is really being able to take like full  advantage  of the diversity of the population in  