lth strategy the  report of the ai for health task force  which we've titled building a learning  health system for canadians  is now available on the cfar website so  kim  let's hear from you a little bit about  why you think it's so important that we  have a national strategy  on ai for health and how it will deliver  benefits to canadians  we really appreciate being able to talk  about this because it was such a  pleasure to  work as part of that task force and you  produced a  fantastic report so congratulations on  that so  i i mean i think it's important to start  just from the the basics  of ai and just my perspective on this is  ai has like many technologies probably  like any  technology at all has a great potential  for good  um but equally can be used to produce  harm and  it could be unintended consequences  still harm  in addition ai is one of the drivers is  what has been described  as the fourth industrial revolution and  should be kind of summarize what i think  people are talking about when they see  this is it's this kind of  continuation of the digital revolution  that's kind of starting to blur  the the distinctions between or to kind  of combine  physical biological and um computing  kind of processes so  um man and machine kind of a framework  of things  so in other words it's expected that ai  is going to be really disruptive  um it to business to government  government to governance  um into our daily lives and if we  believe this  then i think is really important that we  have a coordinated strategy  first of all to make sure that we truly  understand what's happening  and both the unintended and intended  consequences and that we have a handle  on how  best to make sure that we get good  things out of ai  and secondly that we um uh mitigate  any negative consequences if we if we  are able to do so  so then if we think more specifically  about health and the health care system  in canada it makes sense for us to focus  on health in part i mean we have a  ongoing abiding respect and love for our  universal health care system with all  its imperfections it is still a great  building block and hopefully we can  continue to make improvements in that  the ai approaches in health can  accommodate a large amount of data  including images they can help us make  sense of this vast quantity of  information it can improve efficiency  and diagnostics and ai systems  and can be used to augment human  activities so they can  help make providers provide better  services  but they also could do things like help  people live in their  homes for longer and and augment those  informal and formal supports as people  age  so ultimately i think we need a pan  canadian strategy  in ai for health because it is a  disruptive potential  of this technology because of the  changes  in the infrastructure that are needed  for ai are significant  um and because ai and health is  ultimately a global issue  and if we want to to have some influence  on that at a global level we need to  work  in a pan-canadian way yeah absolutely i  think  you know on that last point one of the  things that we discussed a lot at the  task force of course we talked a lot  about data and data access  you'll recall and and other members of  the task force i see are on  this webinar in the audience today we  spent an inordinate  amount of time talking about data and  data access and you know the pandemic  has really  um actually allowed us as a research  and innovation community to make some  important steps and  hdrn has really been leading the way on  that  um but the other thing that we spent a  lot of time talking about  at the task force was really you know  canada's opportunity around the  application of ai to health  and our specific opportunity because we  are you know a great leading ai nation  we have tremendous expertise in this  field across the country  because we have such strong values  around  ai and its use in a responsible and  ethical manner and especially  when we're talking about health and  we're talking about personal data  those principles and values are so  incredibly crucial  but also the opportunity you know to  really leverage that publicly funded  health system that we have and the fact  that the data  is and should be and can be made  available for  research and innovation so um you know  it was  such a such a wide-ranging conversation  and we probably could have had  a hundred recommendations in our report  and we worked so hard to really  narrow them down on the focus of the  things that we felt as a committee  uh were essential first steps in moving  canada forward to really  take advantage of all this and and  bottom line being  you know better health for canadians i  think that's the vision of the task  force so thanks to you for  all of your contributions to to that  work and as i said we've got a couple of  members of the task force i can see our  on our participant list today so thanks  to everybody  for their efforts so let's move to the  audience questions now  and we'll start with a quick one  actually from uh randy gable  uh a colleague of ours on the task force  from  the university of alberta and from amy  our  ai institute in edmonton really quick  question randy asks  is there an earl that provides  information about  research access to health systems covet  test data  randy i think what you're asking for is  how do you find out  uh what data is available about code  testing  through hdrn and the answer to that is  you can go to the hdrn  website and it's all on there and look  for their briefs as well and the brief  today actually  speaks to the metadata that's available  okay so another question that's come in  um  the participant asks what are the most  expected  worries regarding data sharing from the  population  and data owner so really the concern  that citizens have and the concerns that  the public have  about data sharing can you outline some  of that for us can