re potential for harm to come to  individuals or communities  small populations based on that so those  are the things that that the public  worries about  less less blanket or strict yes or no  and more um what's being done to protect  data  and put some guard rails around use  great and uh that five saves framework i  found  in researching this field and working on  the task force was really such  an excellent conceptual model to think  about  what are the things that we have to be  concerned about what are the questions  that we need to be asking ourselves as  researchers  as we're we're considering the use of  individual data  so along the same lines we have a  question that came in from another one  of our participants  about the type of work that you're doing  around community public and patient  engagement  so he asks you to speak more to  community patient  engagement in the hdrn and how that  interfaces with the idea  that individual patient participant  consent is not required in studies of  administrative data  thank you he says yeah great great  question because of course  again the bedrock not all of the data  that we have is this  what we call unconsented data but a big  portion of it is  and and they're unconsented in the sense  that there's allowances for  use of these data in the absence of  consent  um again with the guard rails  around that so when we are talking about  public  and patient engagement in health data  research network canada  we're really we're really focusing on on  data specifically and i contrast that  with the broader strategy for patient  oriented research from cihr which is  what we're funded under  so we're not um so this really is a  focus on  on how are we going to use data what  should we what kind of data should we be  bringing in  and what are the limits to allowances  for research uses of that information  and so we're we have established  an interim public advisory council for  hdr and we're just in the process of  recruiting and filling out a permanent  structure for that and we will be  looking to that group for helping us um  with a number of things one is  communication so we really feel that  transparency is kind of the first order  of business for  the activities that hdrn is doing but  another is really to help  truly create a space where the public  has an influence on the policies  around data use um and then i think  we're gonna  we well we will absolutely take  direction from that advisory committee  on  other things that we should be  considering um  whether that's about information flows  or about doing things differently and  and i do want to emphasize that that we  i i personally feel  very committed to the fact that the  public needs to have a true say in this  this is not just  a pr communications exercise absolutely  and it can't be  uh tokenism in any way it has to be  really deep  engagement consideration collaboration  with the public and with patients and  and we articulated that very strongly in  the task force report very much taking  um inspiration from the work that you've  done at hdrn and that others have done  across the country on this topic really  thinking about  um the role of citizens  in understanding and contributing to  this work going forward  i i was always inspired by uh the uk  where they seem to have a very strong  culture of data trust and i think that's  something that we can learn a lot from  and aspire to build in canada this  culture of  inclusion uh within the governance  within decision making  around data and that's you know that's  how you engender trust and how you're  able to  advance uh research objectives another  couple of quick questions about  hdrn another colleague asks are there  any plans to reach out  beyond our boundaries to coordinate with  other countries  yes as a matter of fact we have  a partnership around covid with the  uk health data research group um  and we're working on a memorandum of  understanding for larger work with them  in fact we're working with them on  metadata now because uh  that with with the idea being that  metadata being that how we describe  the data that we have if we can align  metadata then we are  effectively aligning the potential for  international  collaborative research projects so we're  working with them so that we can  and get those things in place now okay  such a great partner for us because they  have so many similarities in their  health system  in their principles values democracy  um and have been a real leader in this  space so i think the uk is always a  great example to look to  yep uh last question about hdrn and then  we'll start to wrap up  uh for provinces who are looking to  build  their own internal capacity for data  collection and sharing  can or does the hdrn assist them in  accelerating  those improvements that's such a great  question  yeah it's one of the things that we have  found over the last couple years of  really working together more formally  it's  one of the greatest assets we can  provide is the  all the learning and information from  the existing centers to share  with those who are trying to start up  but honestly  equally it goes in the other direction  as well so we have a established a  for example a privacy working group of  all the privacy leads from our  organizations across the country and  they're helping to do  a number of things on a legislative  review and and that sort of thing so  absolutely those resources are there and  we're really open to  helping as we can awesome  so this has been a fabulous conversation  thank you kim for joining me today it's  been  gone by very quickly as as these always  do and i'm afraid we don't  have time for any further questions i  want to remind the audience  that the ai for health task force report  is now available on the cfar website  and before you sign off i'd like to  thank everybody for joining us today for  this conversation and i'd especially  like to thank you kim for telling us the  story of hdr in canada and  the amazing foundation that it has laid  to  integrate and coordinate data across the  country and make it available  for research cfar's virtual talk series  continues  on august 20th with cfar president dr  alan bernstein in conversation with  professor arturo  casa devel from the johns hopkins school  of medicine and he's doing  groundbreaking work on the use of plasma  for the prevention and therapy of covet  19 i hope that you can join us then  please visit our website cfar.ca to find  out more about the series and to view a  recording from today's talk which will  be  posted early next week thanks again to  everybody for joining  us and have a wonderful afternoon  bye-bye

