ability to really  uh analyze and assess and understand  uh the impact on health of canadians  across the country and  the work that you're doing is clearly a  huge step forward from where we have  been in the past so just so that we  understand it a little bit more deeply  so  through hdrn researchers can access data  from across provinces and territories  from across different health systems  and even across different data holders  like statistics canada  as i said it's a huge step forward and  so tell us a little bit about  what are the the key advantages for  researchers to having this kind of  broad multi-jurisdictional access why  have we not been able to do that  in the past and what are the remaining  challenges that you're tackling  yeah great question so i you know start  with why  this has been so difficult um and i  really want to start there by  acknowledging that not everything is  difficult some things have really been  done quite well  so kai ha kai hi has a number of  pan-canadian data sets that are  available and have been available to  researcher for quite some time  and statistics canada clearly has  amazing and  rich data resources and and a research  data center network that has  helped researchers get access to those  and of course a  number of provinces have good really  good data and support for access to  those as well  but having said that it has been  absolutely a  really big challenge and really a loss  to all of us that we haven't been able  to conduct  all of the multi-jurisdictional research  that researchers  are trying to propose and put forward  and  pan canadian projects that extend really  just beyond the data of a single  organization that's where we really  struggle  is working together across organizations  i think there's two kind of fundamental  reasons for this  one one is the nature of our canadian  federation um  and the the tension that that kind of  sets up between provinces and  territories and the  and the federal government and i mean  that's a whole political science  conversation but  i i do think it's important to  acknowledge that that is part of what's  going on here  the other is that because provinces and  territories have  the individual responsibility for  delivery of health care and other kinds  of services  the data to collect the way that they're  collected  and the technical systems that they use  to collect the data  even though the services that are  covered are different  and this really creates a challenge for  again the apples to apples comparison  but also  the the ability just to assemble and  then use the data appropriately  so having said all that the the  advantages that  come from addressing this  um it really  are are that we can we can look at that  the effect it's so it's a natural  laboratory in canada we have different  policies and different practices so we  can learn from those  if we can compare across jurisdictions  we can also at a national level look at  the effects of  small populations small small  number of conditions or people who have  had a  relatively um unique service and really  at the pan canadian level for those  small populations that's the only way we  can do some of this research  and so we do still have lots of  challenges including sharing of data  across  boundaries and at this point i'd also  say that we really haven't had enough  engagement with the public  around at the ethical issues of doing  the kind of data linkage and analysis  that we really want to do  great so and i know that you're working  hard on that front  and and you mentioned earlier the work  that you do in  engaging with indigenous communities as  well one thing that just occurred to me  as you were speaking kim i wonder if  um one of the other advantages of  integrating and coordinating  access to data across the country health  data in particular across the country  is really being able to take like full  advantage  of the diversity of the population in  canada which is distributed  across the country do you see that as  another advantage  oh yes i mean and this is probably  highlighted really with the  the covet pandemic and a lot of the  discussion that's been  happening around vulnerable populations  and communities  race-based data and that sort of thing  if you really want to be able to truly  understand  small populations rural populations  certain kind of  racial and ethnic minority populations  it's probably much better to have  a larger group a larger population  being the canadian population to start  from in those cases  great and i know you know from the very  beginning of the pandemic  hdrn you know pivoted very quickly to  say what can we do specifically  to understand and address covet 19 what  can we do to understand how it's  affecting  canadians how it's affecting health  systems and the demand on health systems  can you give us a couple of examples of  what hdr  and did to address covenant is doing to  address covid and also maybe some of the  specific projects that researchers are  leveraging  uh the hdr and data network in order to  address  yeah let's uh we did recognize early on  that there was  a role for us even as in a starting  point of sharing information  making researchers aware and in one spot  similar to what we've done in other  cases of what data might be available  in which provinces that could support  covid research now in the first instance  that meant documenting where  coveted testing data were going to be  available for research access  and what other kinds of data sets that  those could be linked  to and so we started we put together it  was weekly  at first and we've gone to a little bit  more  less frequent updating of a briefing  note just to describe where we are the  new features that are available  and just more recently i think it's just  today that came out where  we have been working on metadata for the  covid data  and in the coming months i expect that  that will be increasing focus on the  other  data sets that we might be able to link  as we start to think about  research on the um broader effects of  covid  beyond the people who've had the pen or  have had the virus to thinking about the  broader effects on communities and  individuals um  and at this point of course there's no  projects that have gone through the  whole process and been  completed through the hdrn supports but  we've been working with  a lot of researchers on pursuing  potential topics or studies they might  want to do  and working with some specific ones on  on  funded projects now ranging from  emergency department use  and in that case we're working with a  team that's actually assembling a new  data set that will be available to  others as well  and to virtual care reproductive care  and pregnancy outcomes  so the wide range of things sort of the  the full spectrum of what  what we can do with the the data that we  have i know as a  sort of an interested stakeholder in the  ecosystem i've really appreciated  getting  your covet 19 briefing notes on a  regular basis to  just maintain my awareness about what's  going on and what's available  for researchers so that i can share it  amongst my community as well  uh i think we're uh just about ready to  head into questions so i just want to  let the audience know that we'll head to  questions  after uh our next topic of discussion so  if you've got questions please put them  into  uh the q a box for us and we'll get to  them right away but the last  topic that i want to touch on sort of  formally with kim  is the fact that over the last year she  and i have worked together and had the  opportunity to work with  15 other leaders from across the ai and  health  research innovation ecosystems in a  national  ai for health task force and what we've  been doing  is developing a series of  recommendations to both the federal  government  and provincial and territorial  governments towards  a national ai for health strategy the  report of the ai for health task force  which we've titled building a learning  health system for canadians  is now available on the cfar website so  kim  let's hear from you a little bit about  why you think it's so important that we  have a national strategy  on ai for health and how it will deliver  benefits to canadians  we really appreciate being able to talk  about this because it was such a  pleasure to  work as part of that task force and you  produced a  fantastic report so congratulations on  that so  i i mean i think it's important to start  just from the the basics  of ai and just my perspective on this is  ai has like many technologies probably  like any  technology at all has a great potential  for good  um but equally can be used to produce  harm and  it could be unintended consequences  still harm  in addition ai is one of the drivers is  what has been described  as the fourth industrial revolution and  should be kind of summarize what i think  people are talking about when they see  this is it's this kind of  continuation of the digital revolution  that's kind of starting to blur  the the distinctions between or to kind  of combine  physical biological and um computing  kind of processes so  um man and machine kind of a framework  of things  so in other words it's expected that ai  is going to be really disruptive  um it to business to government  government to governance  um into our daily lives and if we  believe this  then i think is really important that we  have a coordinated strategy  first of all to make sure that we truly  understand what's happening  and both the unintended and intended  consequences and that we have a handle  on how  best to make sure that we get good  things out of ai  and secondly that we um uh mitigate  any negative consequences if we if we  are able to do so  so then if we think more specifically  about health and the health care system  in canada it makes sense for us to focus  on health in part i mean we have a  ongoing abiding respect and love for our  universal health care system with all  its imperfections it is still a great  building block and hopefully we can  continue to make improvements in that  the ai approaches in health can  accommodate a large amount of data  including images they can help us make  sense of this vast quantity of  information it can improve efficiency  and diagnostics and ai systems  and can be used to augment human  activities so they can  help make providers provide better  services  but they also could do things like help  people live in their  homes for longer and and augment those  informal and formal supports as people  age  so ultimately i think we need a pan  canadian strategy  in ai for health because it is a  disruptive potential  of this technology because of the  changes  in the infrastructure that are needed  for ai are significant  um and because ai and health is  ultimately a global issue  and if we want to to have some influence  on that at a global level we need to  work  in a pan-canadian way yeah absolutely i  think  you know on that last point one of the  things that we discussed a lot at the  task force of course we talked a lot  about data and data access  you'll recall and and other members of  the task force i see are on  this webinar in the audience today we  spent an inordinate  amount of time talking about data and  data access and you know the pandemic  has really  um actually allowed us as a research  and innovation community to make some  important steps and  hdrn has really been leading the way on  that  um but the other thing that we spent a  lot of time talking about  at the task force was really you know  canada's opportunity around the  application of ai to health  and our specific opportunity because we  are you know a great leading ai nation  we have tremendous expertise in this  field across the country  because we have such strong values  around  ai and its use in a responsible and  ethical manner and especially  when we're talking about health and  we're talking about personal data  those principles and values are so  incredibly crucial  but also the opportunity you know to  really leverage that publicly funded  health system that we have and the fact  that the data  is and should be and can be made  available for  research and innovation so um you know  it was  such a such a wide-ranging conversation  and we probably could have had  a hundred recommendations in our report  and we worked so hard to really  narrow them down on the focus of the  things that we felt as a committee  uh were essential first steps in moving  canada forward to really  take advantage of all this and and  bottom line being  you know better health for canadians i  think that's the vision of the task  force so thanks to you for  all of your contributions to to that  work and as i said we've got a couple of  members of the task force i can see our  on our participant list today so thanks  to everybody  for their efforts so let's move to the  audience questions now  and we'll start with a quick one  actually from uh randy gable  uh a colleague of ours on the task force  from  the university of alberta and from amy  our  ai institute in edmonton really quick  question randy asks  is there an earl that provides  information about  research access to health systems covet  test data  randy i think what you're asking for is  how do you find out  uh what data is available about code  testing  through hdrn and the answer to that is  you can go to the hdrn  website and it's all on there and look  for their briefs as well and the brief  today actually  speaks to the metadata that's available  okay so another question that's come in  um  the participant asks what are the most  expected  worries regarding data sharing from the  population  and data owner so really the concern  that citizens have and the concerns that  the public have  about data sharing can you outline some  of that for us can please  sure you know this is a  it's a difficult question to answer in  part because i don't want to pretend to  speak on behalf of  all canadians what i can speak to is the  existing literature that  has resulted from a pretty deep public  deliberation  and including focus groups but longer  deliberation sessions as well and  i have been leading some of those in in  british columbia but there's a  international group of people who've  been working in this area and  interestingly there's quite a lot of  consistency across countries  from what we can see in this so people  are really really open to having their  data  used for research um but it's always  in context with provisos and those  provisos really  revolve around this uh the five safes  framework um and this this is a  framework that's been  emerging as just it doesn't say what  needs to be done  but it tells you what you need to think  about and what those things are  is safe people so who is it that's  acting  accessing the data and people have a lot  of trust for  researchers university-based researchers  they start to get  a little more concerned if we're talking  about private industry  and it's not i don't want to say that  it's a hard no it's just that it changes  the conversation a bit and there's  different considerations  so safe people is a big one safe  environments where are you going to be  doing  the research and increasingly people  expect things to be done in a controlled  secure environment because that just  says that you've got greater control  over what's happening  um safe projects so what is the question  that's being  asked and who's vetted it to say that it  has scientific merit and will produce  some public value  safe data so this is the uh how  identifying are the data that are going  to be used and have things been done to  at least remove direct identifiers names  and addresses and things like that  and safe outputs what's coming out  what's going to be shared publicly  is there potential for harm to come to  individuals or communities  small populations based on that so those  are the things that that the public  worries about  less less blanket or strict yes or no  and more um what's being done to protect  data  and put some guard rails around use  great and uh that five saves framework i  found  in researching this field and working on  the task force was really such  an excellent conceptual model to think  about  what are the things that we have to be  concerned about what are the questions  that we need to be asking ourselves as  researchers  as we're we're considering the use of  individual data  so along the same lines we have a  question that came in from another one  of our participants  about the type of work that you're doing  around community public and patient  engagement  so he asks you to speak more to  community patient  engagement in the hdrn and how that  interfaces with the idea  that individual patient participant  consent is not required in studies of  administrative data  thank you he says yeah great great  question because of course  again the bedrock not all of the data  that we have is this  what we call unconsented data but a big  portion of it is  and and they're unconsented in the sense  that there's allowances for  use of these data in the absence of  consent  um again with the guard rails  around that so when we are talking about  public  and patient engagement in health data  research network canada  we're really we're really focusing on on  data specifically and i contrast that  with the broader strategy for patient  oriented research from cihr which is  what we're funded under  so we're not um so this really is a  focus on  on how are we going to use data what  should we what kind of data should we be  bringing in  and what are the limits to allowances  for research uses of that information  and so we're we have established  an interim public advisory council for  hdr and we're just in the process of  recruiting and filling out a permanent  structure for that and we will be  looking to that group for helping us um  with a number of things one is  communication so we really feel that  transparency is kind of the first order  of business for  the activities that hdrn is doing but  another is really to help  truly create a space where the public  has an influence on the policies  around data use um and then i think  we're gonna  we well we will absolutely take  direction from that advisory committee  on  other things that we should be  considering um  whether that's about information flows  or about doing things differently and  and i do want to emphasize that that we  i i personally feel  very committed to the fact that the  public needs to have a true say in this  this is not just  a pr communications exercise absolutely  and it can't be  uh tokenism in any way it has to be  really deep  engagement consideration collaboration  with the public and with patients and  and we articulated that very strongly in  the task force report very much taking  um inspiration from the work that you've  done at hdrn and that others have done  across the country on this topic really  thinking about  um the role of citizens  in understanding and contributing to  this work going forward  i i was always inspired by uh the uk  where they seem to have a very strong  culture of data trust and i think that's  something that we can learn a lot from  and aspire to build in canada this  culture of  inclusion uh within the governance  within decision making  around data and that's you know that's  how you engender trust and how you're  able to  advance uh research objectives another  couple of quick questions about  hdrn another colleague asks are there  any plans to reach out  beyond our boundaries to coordinate with  other countries  yes as a matter of fact we have  a partnership around covid with the  uk health data research group um  and we're working on a memorandum of  understanding for larger work with them  in fact we're working with them on  metadata now because uh  that with with the idea being that  metadata being that how we describe  the data that we have if we can align  metadata then we are  effectively aligning the potential for  international  collaborative research projects so we're  working with them so that we can  and get those things in place now okay  such a great partner for us because they  have so many similarities in their  health system  in their principles values democracy  um and have been a real leader in this  space so i think the uk is always a  great example to look to  yep uh last question about hdrn and then  we'll start to wrap up  uh for provinces who are looking to  build  their own internal capacity for data  collection and sharing  can or does the hdrn assist them in  accelerating  those improvements that's such a great  question  yeah it's one of the things that we have  found over the last couple years of  really working together more formally  it's  one of the greatest assets we can  provide is the  all the learning and information from  the existing centers to share  with those who are trying to start up  but honestly  equally it goes in the other direction  as well so we have a established a  for example a privacy working group of  all the privacy leads from our  organizations across the country and  they're helping to do  a number of things on a legislative  review and and that sort of thing so  absolutely those resources are there and  we're really open to  helping as we can awesome  so this has been a fabulous conversation  thank you kim for joining me today it's  been  gone by very quickly as as these always  do and i'm afraid we don't  have time for any further questions i  want to remind the audience  that the ai for health task force report  is now available on the cfar website  and before you sign off i'd like to  thank everybody for joining us today for  this conversation and i'd especially  like to thank you kim for telling us the  story of hdr in canada and  the amazing foundation that it has laid  to  integrate and coordinate data across the  country and make it available  for research cfar's virtual talk series  continues  on august 20th with cfar president dr  alan bernstein in conversation with  professor arturo  casa devel from the johns hopkins school  of medicine and he's doing  groundbreaking work on the use of plasma  for the prevention and therapy of covet  19 i hope that you can join us then  please visit our website cfar.ca to find  out more about the series and to view a  recording from today's talk which will  be  posted early next week thanks again to  everybody for joining  us and have a wonderful afternoon  bye-bye
 

